// Three fictional Harborline Software blog posts used as sample sources (all original text)
const SAMPLES = [
  { id: 0, title: 'How a nine-person accountancy cut client onboarding from three weeks to four days', text: `How a nine-person accountancy cut client onboarding from three weeks to four days

Marlow & Finch is a fictional accountancy practice in Bristol with nine staff and about a dozen new clients a month. Until this spring, onboarding a client meant a welcome call, a PDF checklist sent by email, and then two to three weeks of chasing: bank statements that arrived as photos, ID documents that were the wrong side, an engagement letter that sat unsigned in someone's inbox. One partner estimated the team spent six hours per client just asking for things.

We rebuilt the process around a single intake form and a small set of automations. The client fills in one form that adapts to their business type. Every answer lands in the practice's CRM, and the system immediately creates a shared folder with named upload slots: "Bank statements (last 3 months)", "Photo ID", "Proof of address". When a slot stays empty for 48 hours, the client gets a short, friendly reminder that names the exact document. Partners see a board with every open client and the documents still missing.

The engagement letter is generated from the form answers and sent for e-signature the same day. When the signature comes back, the client's bookkeeping software is set up from a template and the first review call is booked from the partner's live calendar.

The numbers after two months: median onboarding time fell from 19 days to 4, reminder emails written by hand dropped to zero, and the team recovered roughly 55 hours a month. Clients noticed too; three of them mentioned the "upload slots" in their first review.

Three lessons for other small practices. First, the bottleneck was never the accountants, it was the waiting; make the next step obvious for the client and the time disappears. Second, reminders work when they are specific. "Please send your documents" gets ignored; "We still need your October bank statement" gets a reply within the hour. Third, start with the one form. Everything else hangs off the data it collects.

If your practice onboards more than five clients a month and still chases documents by email, this is the first automation worth building.` },
  { id: 1, title: 'Five automations every small online shop should run before the holiday rush', text: `Five automations every small online shop should run before the holiday rush

Every November the same thing happens to small online shops: orders triple, the support inbox fills with "where is my order?", and the two people who run the store spend their evenings copying tracking numbers into emails. The fix is not more staff for six weeks; it is a handful of automations that take an afternoon each to set up.

1. Order-status replies that answer themselves. Connect the store and the carrier to a support assistant that recognises "where is my order" questions, looks up the order by email address or order number, and replies with the live tracking status. Anything it cannot answer goes to a human with the order details already attached. Shops that run this see roughly 40 percent of holiday tickets closed without anyone touching them.

2. Abandoned-cart messages that sound like a person. A WhatsApp or email nudge 45 minutes after a cart is left behind, written in the shop's own voice, with a picture of the item and one line that answers the usual hesitation (delivery time, returns, sizing). No discount in the first message; keep that for a second nudge the next day if it is still unpaid.

3. Low-stock alerts to the buyer, not the dashboard. Nobody checks the inventory report on a Saturday night. A rule that messages the person who reorders when a bestseller drops under seven days of cover, with the supplier's email already drafted, prevents the worst holiday problem: selling something you no longer have.

4. Review requests timed to delivery. Send the review request two days after the carrier confirms delivery, not two days after the order. The difference in reply rate is large, and the reviews mention the product rather than the delivery.

5. A daily owner summary. One message at 8 am: yesterday's orders and revenue, refunds, tickets still open, items low on stock, and the three products that moved most. It replaces an hour of clicking through four dashboards.

None of these needs a developer on staff. They need the store's data (orders, carriers, stock) connected once, and a plain-English rule for each situation. Set them up in October, test them on a quiet week, and let them run while you pack boxes.` },
  { id: 2, title: 'What a lead that goes cold really costs an estate agency', text: `What a lead that goes cold really costs an estate agency

An estate agency we will call Harbour View Property (fictional) gets about 120 enquiries a month from portals, its website and social ads. The agents are good at their jobs. The problem is the time between the enquiry and the first reply. On a weekday it averaged four hours; on a weekend it was often the following Monday. By then the buyer had spoken to two other agencies and booked viewings with one of them.

We measured it for a month. Enquiries answered within five minutes booked a viewing 52 percent of the time. Enquiries answered after an hour booked a viewing 19 percent of the time. With an average fee of 1.2 percent on a 380,000 sale, each lost viewing is worth real money, and the agency was losing dozens of viewings a month to speed alone.

The fix was not a call centre. It was an instant, honest first reply plus routing. Every enquiry, whichever channel it comes from, gets an answer within a minute: a thank-you, the three facts people ask about (is it still available, when can I see it, what are the running costs), and a question that qualifies the lead: buying or renting, timeline, mortgage agreed or not. The answers go into the CRM, the enquiry is assigned to the agent who covers that street, and the agent gets a WhatsApp message with a one-line summary and the suggested next action. If nobody picks it up within 30 minutes, it goes to the office manager.

Two months later the median first reply is under two minutes at any hour, viewings booked from enquiries rose by a third, and agents say the best part is that they no longer start the day with forty unread portal emails; they start with a short list of people who are ready to view.

What this is not: it is not a bot that pretends to be an agent and books viewings on its own. People can tell, and it damages trust. The first reply says plainly that an agent will follow up, and the agent does.

If you want to know what your own agency is losing, pull last month's enquiries, note the time of the first reply for each, and count the viewings. The pattern will be the same.` },
